<span class="kicker">Mechanism</span>
<h1>Where the number goes after the overflow stops</h1>
<p class="lede">My readings say the water is dirty after rain. They do not say why, and they do not say for how long. This page is my attempt to write the why down as a mass balance, solve it, and find out how much of the answer I actually know.</p>

<h2 id="question">The question this page is for</h2>
<p>The advisory tells you what the water was like last Saturday morning. The rain and risk model on the <a class='tx' href='/methods/#model'>methods page</a> tells you how likely tomorrow is to be over the 235 line. Neither one answers the question a coach actually asks, which is: it poured on Saturday night, can we row on Monday.</p>
<p>The answer this project has been giving is forty eight hours, because that is the caution window everyone uses after wet weather. EPA's 2012 recreational criteria are the guidance underneath it, and they say that if a state is not sampling during or right after rain, it <q>should consider advising the public of the potential additional risk of primary contact recreation when sources such as urban runoff or CSOs may be impairing water quality.</q> That is a recommendation to warn people. It is not a length. Nowhere in the guidance, and nowhere on New Jersey's own combined sewer notification system, did I find a number of hours.</p>
<p>So I have been repeating a number I could not explain. This page is me trying to derive it instead.</p>

<h2 id="source">What actually reaches this dock</h2>
<p>The combined sewer permittee upstream of us is the City of Paterson, NJPDES NJ0108880. The permit's cover letter says the city <q>owns/operates twenty-three (23) Combined Sewer Overflow (CSO) outfalls</q> and that <q>These CSO outfalls discharge into the Passaic River</q>. Every one of them is above the Dundee Dam.</p>
<p>They are not rare. The Passaic Valley Sewerage Commission's System Characterization Report models each Paterson outfall over an approved typical year and gives frequencies from 4 to 56 overflows a year, with a median of 30. The same report says that <q>Under existing conditions, CSO occurs in all communities at a storm depth of 0.2 to 0.3 inches.</q> A fifth of an inch of rain is enough.</p>
<p>Because the outfalls are all above the dam, everything they send down has to come over the dam to get to us. That is genuinely useful, because there is a USGS gauge on the dam, 01389890, and it has been publishing daily discharge since 2007. The top of my reach has a flow meter on it.</p>

<h2 id="box">The reach as one box</h2>
<p>I am going to treat the whole four miles from the dam to the dock as a single well mixed box. That is a strong assumption and I come back to it at the bottom of the page. It buys me something worth having: with one box, only three things can change the number of E. coli in the reach.</p>
<ul class="bul">
<li>Bacteria arrive, carried in over the dam.</li>
<li>Bacteria leave, carried out the bottom end past the dock.</li>
<li>Bacteria die where they are.</li>
</ul>
<p>Nothing else. If I can write those three down, I have closed the budget, and the rest is calculus.</p>
