ace" font-size="10.5" fill="#4A555C" letter-spacing="0.08em" text-anchor="end">OUT</text><line x1="446" y1="348" x2="446" y2="388" stroke="#A3231C" stroke-width="2.4"/><path d="M446 388 l-5 -9 l10 0 z" fill="#A3231C"/><text x="458" y="368" font-family="IBM Plex Mono, ui-monospace, monospace" font-size="12" fill="#A3231C" font-weight="600">k V C</text><text x="458" y="383" font-family="IBM Plex Sans, Helvetica, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#4A555C">bacteria dying where they are, per second</text><text x="10" y="368" font-family="IBM Plex Sans, Helvetica, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#4A555C">Nothing else is allowed to happen</text><text x="10" y="381" font-family="IBM Plex Sans, Helvetica, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#4A555C">in this picture. That is what makes</text><text x="10" y="394" font-family="IBM Plex Sans, Helvetica, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#4A555C">it a budget and not a story, and it</text><text x="10" y="407" font-family="IBM Plex Sans, Helvetica, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#4A555C">is also what makes it wrong in the</text><text x="10" y="420" font-family="IBM Plex Sans, Helvetica, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#4A555C">specific ways listed further down.</text></svg></div><figcaption>The three steps, and the control volume they add up to. A loading term, a dilution term, and a reach that loses bacteria two ways at once. The die-off arrow is drawn leaving the bottom of the box because those organisms do not go anywhere; they stop being counted.</figcaption></figure>

<h2 id="equation">The equation, and solving it</h2>
<p>Write the budget for the total number of organisms in the reach, which is the volume times the concentration.</p>
<div class="mono" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); padding:12px 0 12px 18px; margin-top:16px; font-size:14px; line-height:2.0; white-space:pre-wrap; max-width:none; overflow-x:auto">V dC/dt  =  Q C_in  -  Q C  -  k V C</div>
<p>Divide every term by V, and notice that the two loss terms have the same shape. Both are a number times C.</p>
<div class="mono" style="border-left:3px solid var(--border); padding:12px 0 12px 18px; margin-top:16px; font-size:14px; line-height:2.0; white-space:pre-wrap; max-width:none; overflow-x:auto">dC/dt  =  (Q/V) C_in  -  (Q/V + k) C</div>
<p>Q/V has units of one over time. Its reciprocal, &tau; = V/Q, is the flushing time of the reach: how long the water itself takes to get from the dam to the dock. So call the two loss rates together</p>
<div class="mono" style="border-left:3px solid var(--border); padding:12px 0 12px 18px; margin-top:16px; font-size:14px; line-height:2.0; white-space:pre-wrap; max-width:none; overflow-x:auto">&lambda;  =  1/&tau;  +  k</div>
<p>That single line is the whole physical idea of this page, and it is worth saying in English before doing any more algebra. <b>The reach cleans itself two ways at the same time, by flushing and by dying, and the faster of the two is the one that matters.</b> They add. Neither one has to be fast on its own.</p>

<h3>Phase one, while the overflow is running</h3>
<p>During the overflow the load is a constant, so with W standing for the whole input term the equation is dC/dt = W/V &minus; &lambda;C. Every term on the right depends only on C, so it separates.</p>
<div class="mono" style="border-left:3px solid var(--border); padding:12px 0 12px 18px; margin-top:16px; font-size:13.5px; line-height:2.0; white-space:pre-wrap; max-width:none; overflow-x:auto">          dC / (W/V - &lambda;C)  =  dt

integrate both sides

    -(1/&lambda;) ln|W/V - &lambda;C|  =  t + A

exponentiate and rearrange, writing C_ss = W/(&lambda;V)

           C(t)  =  C_ss + (C_0 - C_ss) e^(-&lambda;t)</div>
<p>C_ss is the ceiling. Set dC/dt to zero in the original equation and you get the same thing: if the overflow ran forever, the concentration would climb towards W/(&lambda;V) and stop there. It never overshoots. That is the first thing the model tells you that a correlation cannot.</p>

<h3>Phase two, after it stops</h3>
<p>When the overflow ends, W becomes zero and the equation collapses to the simplest one there is.</p>
<div class="mono" style="border-left:3px solid var(--border); padding:12px 0 12px 18px; margin-top:16px; font-size:13.5px; line-height:2.0; white-space:pre-wrap; max-width:none; overflow-x:auto">        dC/dt  =  -&lambda;C

              dC/C  =  -&lambda; dt

           ln C  =  -&lambda;t + A

           C(t)  =  C_0 e^(-&lambda;t)</div>
<p>Now ask the actual question. How long until the concentration falls back to the 235 CFU/100 mL line the advisory calls red at? Set C equal to 235 and solve for t.</p>
<p class="note">Two published lines run through this project and this page uses only one of them. <b>235 CFU/100 mL</b> is EPA's Beach Action Value and is the line the advisory calls red at, so it is the line worth asking how long the water stays above. <b>126 CFU/100 mL</b> is the geometric mean criterion, the line a Saturday has to stay under to be called green, and it is the line the rain and risk model on the <a class='tx' href='/methods/#model'>methods page</a> is scored against. Both are set out on the <a class='tx' href='/methods/#criteria'>methods page</a>. Neither is "the line" on its own.</p>
<div class="mono" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent); padding:12px 0 12px 18px; margin-top:16px; font-size:14px; line-height:2.0; white-space:pre-wrap; max-width:none; overflow-x:auto">   t  =  ln(C_0 / 235) / &lambda;        =        ln(C_0 / 235) / (1/&tau; + k)</div>
<p>That is the answer to the question at the top of the page, and it is worth looking at for a moment, because it says two things I did not expect.</p>
<p><b>First, the size of the overflow barely matters.</b> C_0 only enters through a logarithm. Making the spill fifty five times stronger, which is the whole distance from the street runoff end of a combined sewer to the raw sewage end, only stretches the window by a factor of about four. So all the effort I could put into pinning down exactly how much sewage came out would buy me very little.</p>
<p><b>Second, &lambda; is in the denominator, so it does all the work.</b> And &lambda; is the sum of a term I can look up on a gauge and a term nobody can pin down. That is where the rest of this page goes.</p>

<h2 id="parameters">The parameters, and where every one came from</h2>
<p>Four of these are measured, one is arithmetic on two published tables, and two are mine. I have marked which is which, because a model whose weak numbers are hidden is worse than no model.</p>
