<p>Two honest notes on that table. The cross sections are measured at the gauge, which sits at the dam, not down in the reach below it; nobody publishes a surveyed profile for the four miles I care about, so I am using the nearest real geometry there is. And the set of 23 outfalls PVSC modeled is not quite the set of 23 the permit lists: the model includes outfall PT028 and leaves out PT033.</p>

<h2 id="dilution">Dilution is not what protects this water</h2>
<p>Before solving anything, one number is worth putting on its own. EPA's combined sewer overflow monitoring guidance says, in a footnote, that <q>In areas where the receiving water is used for swimming, the dilution needs to be at least 10,000 to 1 for bacteria.</q></p>
<p>At the median September flow this reach gives about <b>5 to 1</b>. At the lowest autumn flow in the gauge record it gives about <b>3 to 1</b>. Even at a thousand cubic feet a second it is only about 18 to 1. We are short of that guidance number by a factor of roughly two thousand.</p>
<p>That is not a scandal, it is just what a small river under a city looks like, and the guidance is written for beaches. But it settles something about the mechanism. Whatever makes the water at this dock safe again after a storm, it is not that the river is big enough to swallow the overflow. The river is not remotely big enough. What makes it safe again is time, which means &lambda;, which means the two terms in &lambda; are the entire story.</p>

<h2 id="solution">What the solution looks like</h2>
