e river, three different values of one coefficient. The window over the line is 5 hours, 39 hours or 88 hours depending on which published value of the die-off rate you pick, and all three are values you can find in the literature for temperate water. The curve shape is the analytical solution derived above, not a numerical simulation.</figcaption></figure>
<p>That figure is the problem in one picture. The mechanism is settled: rise, ceiling, exponential decay. The <i>number</i> is not settled at all, and the whole spread comes from one coefficient.</p>

<h2 id="sweep">The sweep, which is the real result</h2>
<p>So the honest thing to do is stop pretending I have a value of k and sweep it across everything the literature says it could be.</p>
<p>The standard reference is Bowie and others, <i>Rates, Constants, and Kinetics Formulations in Surface Water Quality Modeling</i>, EPA/600/3-85/040, and its chapter 8 is entirely about coliform disappearance. Its numbers are per hour, so everything below is multiplied by 24.</p>
<ul class="bul">
<li><b>Table 8-2</b> collects 30 studies of freshwater rates measured in the field. The report's own summary: <q>The median rate for the in situ studies is .04 hr-1 with 60 percent of the values less than .05 hr-1 and 90 percent less than .22 hr-1.</q> That median is 0.96 per day, and the 90th percentile is 5.3 per day.</li>
<li><b>Table 8-3</b> collects values used in actual modeling studies, from 0.0004 to 0.333 per hour, which is 0.01 to 8.0 per day. The single widest entry in it, 0.02 to 0.333 per hour, is for the Long Island estuaries in New York, which is the nearest thing in the table to the water I am standing in.</li>
<li><b>Table 8-9</b> is the one that changed how I think about this. It gives the time for a 90 percent die-off, hour by hour through a single day at a single site: 40 hours at three in the morning, 2.3 hours at eleven. Same water, same day, factor of seventeen, because of sunlight.</li>
</ul>
<p>The compendium draws its own conclusion, and I am going to quote it rather than paraphrase it, because it is the reason this page has a sweep in it instead of an answer: <q>It is clear that estimating a single value for a k could result in greater than order-of-magnitude errors.</q></p>
<p>One thing I went looking for and did not find is worth reporting as an absence. I could not locate a die-off coefficient measured on the Lower Passaic, or on any New Jersey tidal river, and the total maximum daily load documents I checked for other rivers name the die-off step without publishing the number they used for it. So there is no local value to borrow. That is a real gap, not a gap in my searching, and it is the reason this page ends with a measurement I would have to make myself rather than a citation I could have looked up.</p>
<p>One more thing pushes the range wider here specifically. NJDEP classifies this stretch, the Passaic from the Dundee Lake dam to the confluence with the Second River, as FW2-NT/SE2. SE2 is a saline estuarine class. Bowie's chapter says plainly that <q>Rates of disappearance are also sensitive to the salinity of the water</q>, and salt speeds die-off up. This reach sits at the fresh end of a tidal river, which puts it between the two families of published numbers rather than inside either.</p>
