<h3>Where the middle of that bracket comes from</h3>
<p>I did not want the central value to be something I picked because it made the answer come out. So it is worth saying that four independent lines land in the same place. Bowie's median of 30 field studies is 0.96 per day. His Table 8-6, a separate compilation of 13 reported fecal coliform rates, gives a median of 1.15. Servais and others calibrated a first-order rate of 0.045 per hour, which is 1.08 per day, across the whole Seine drainage network, a temperate lowland river with a lot in common with this one. And the dark freshwater term in Mancini's relationship is 0.8 per day at 20 degrees. Four different methods, four different decades, all between 0.8 and 1.2.</p>
<p>For the slow end there is a measured field number rather than a compilation. Sinton and others held E. coli in outdoor 300 litre chambers at 14 degrees C, which is close to what this river runs at in late October, and got a dark rate of 0.0171 per hour, or <b>0.41 per day</b>, a T-90 of 135 hours. Their sunlit chambers ran more than ten times faster, and got faster again by a factor of about 2.5 going from fresh water to sea water. That is the spread in a single controlled experiment.</p>

<h3>What this season's own thermometer says</h3>
<p>There is one more thing I can do here without leaving my own data. Mancini's relationship, as EPA's compendium reproduces it, makes the dark part of the die-off rate a function of temperature:</p>
<div class="mono" style="border-left:3px solid var(--border); padding:12px 0 12px 18px; margin-top:16px; font-size:14px; line-height:2.0; white-space:pre-wrap; max-width:none; overflow-x:auto">k_dark  =  (0.8 + 0.006 x percent seawater) x 1.07^(T - 20)   per day</div>
<p>The 1.07 is an Arrhenius factor, so the rate roughly doubles for every ten degrees. I record water temperature at the dock every week, so I can just put my own numbers in it.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table class="tbl tight">
<caption>The dark die-off floor at this station's measured water temperatures</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Saturday</th><th class="n" scope="col">Water temp</th><th class="n" scope="col">k in the dark</th><th class="n" scope="col">T-90</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>22 August 2026</td><td class="n">24.8 C</td><td class="n">1.11 /day</td><td class="n">50 h</td></tr>
<tr><td>19 September 2026</td><td class="n">21.0 C</td><td class="n">0.86 /day</td><td class="n">65 h</td></tr>
<tr><td>24 October 2026</td><td class="n">14.5 C</td><td class="n">0.55 /day</td><td class="n">100 h</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The salinity term barely matters: even at five percent seawater it adds 0.03 per day. Temperature is doing the work, and so is daylight, which this relationship treats separately and which an overcast morning does not supply.</p>
<p>Read that against the two thresholds from the sweep, 0.75 and 0.98 per day. In August the dark floor alone clears both. By the last Saturday of the season it is below both. The temperature column I have been writing on a clipboard all autumn, plugged into a relationship published in 1978, says the same thing the sweep says.</p>

<h2 id="finding">What this says about the 48 hour rule</h2>
<p>Reading the crossings off that figure gives one plain sentence, and it is the finding of this page.</p>
<p class="pull">At the river's ordinary autumn flow, the 48 hour rule holds only if E. coli is dying faster than about 0.75 per day, and at the lowest autumn flow on the gauge record only above about 1 per day, which is almost exactly the median of the 30 published field studies.</p>
<p>In other words, 48 hours is not a safety margin with room in it. It is roughly a coin flip against the literature. Half the published field measurements of die-off are slow enough that, at low flow, the water at this dock would still be over the 235 line when the two days are up.</p>
<p>It also says clearly when the rule is fine, and that half is genuinely reassuring. At a thousand cubic feet a second the curve never touches the 48 hour line at any k in the range: the reach flushes itself out in eleven hours and the die-off does not need to do anything. Big storms cause big overflows, but big storms also raise the river, and the flushing term rises with it. The rule works most of the time because the two things are correlated.</p>
<p>The case it fails is the one that does not feel dangerous. A modest storm, a fifth of an inch, enough to start the outfalls but not enough to lift the river. Cool, overcast water, so the sunlight term in the die-off is small. That is a late October morning on this river, which is exactly the end of my own season, and it is the case where my own temperature record puts the dark die-off floor at 0.55 per day, under both thresholds.</p>
<p>I want to be careful about what I am claiming. This is a calculation, not a measurement. It did not change any advisory I published, and none of the ten Saturdays on this site is a recorded overflow event, so there is nothing here I have tested against. What the calculation gives me is a reason, and a specific condition to watch for, and a list of the things I would have to measure to find out whether it is right.</p>
<p>One check I can do is a sanity check on scale. The model's peak at the dock, for a mid sized overflow at the median flow, comes out in the low thousands of CFU/100 mL. My own wet weather Saturdays ran from 420 to 2,400. Those are the same order of magnitude, which tells me the assumed load is not absurd. It is not a validation and I am not going to call it one: the model was never fitted to those readings, and not one of them is known to follow an overflow.</p>

<h2 id="assumptions">Every assumption, and what breaks it</h2>
<p>In order of how much they worry me.</p>
<ul class="bul">
<li><b>No tidal reversal inside the window.</b> This is the one I think is wrong. The reach is tidal, the dock is four miles below the head of tide, and the flood tide pushes water back upstream twice a day. Treating the outflow as Q, the freshwater discharge, assumes a parcel of water leaves and does not come back. In a real tidal reach it sloshes, and the same water passes the dock several times before it is gone. <b>What it costs:</b> the true flushing time at low flow is longer than V/Q, maybe substantially, so 1/&tau; is too big, so &lambda; is too big, so <b>the exceedance windows on this page are too short.</b> The error runs in the unsafe direction. If I could fix one thing, it would be this one.</li>
<li><b>The whole reach is one well mixed box.</b> Four miles of river does not become uniform instantly. A real overflow arrives as a slug that spreads as it travels, so the actual concentration at the dock is a sharper, higher, briefer peak arriving some hours after the overflow, not a smooth reach wide rise. <b>What it costs:</b> the peak is understated and its timing is smeared. The total exposure is roughly preserved, which is why the box model is still worth writing, but nobody should read the timing of the rise off this page.</li>
<li><b>Constant discharge through the event.</b> Q is held at one value while the real river rises during the storm and falls back over the next day or two. <b>What it costs:</b> the flushing term is highest right at the start and decays afterwards, so a fixed Q overstates late flushing. This also pushes the real answer longer than the model's.</li>
<li><b>No resuspension, and no sediment store.</b> Bacteria settle into the bed and come back up when the water moves. Bowie's own Table 8-1 lists sedimentation first among the factors affecting coliform rates. <b>What it costs:</b> a real decay curve has a tail the exponential does not, so the water can stay marginal longer than the model says.</li>
<li><b>One point source.</b> There are 23 outfalls, not one, spread along Paterson, and there is street runoff entering all along the reach from places that are not combined sewers at all. <b>What it costs:</b> the model cannot attribute anything. It says what an overflow of this size would do; it cannot say that an overflow is what happened.</li>
<li><b>Decay is a single exponential.</b> It is probably not. Phelps fitted Ohio River data as two populations rather than one: about 99.5 percent of the organisms disappearing at roughly 1.08 per day and the surviving half a percent at 0.13, a rate eight times slower. <b>What it costs:</b> a single exponential over-predicts how much is gone at long travel times, so the real tail of an event sits above my curve. This is a second, independent reason the windows here are more likely short than long.</li>
<li><b>k is constant.</b> It is not, and Table 8-9 is the proof: it moves by a factor of seventeen between three in the morning and eleven. Over a 48 hour window it goes through two full cycles of that. <b>What it costs:</b> a single k is a two day average of a quantity that never sits still, which is precisely why this page sweeps it instead of choosing it.</li>
<li><b>The straight line distance.</b> The river meanders, so the real path from the dam to the dock is longer than 6.40 km, which means the real reach volume is larger than 1.09 million m<sup>3</sup>. <b>What it costs:</b> a bigger V is a longer &tau;, which again means the real windows are longer than the ones printed here. Three of the seven assumptions push the same way, and it is not the safe way.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="not">What this is not</h2>
<ul class="bul">
<li>It is not a measurement of an overflow. I have never sampled during one.</li>
<li>It is not calibrated. Every parameter is published, computed or assumed, and none was tuned to make an answer come out.</li>
<li>It did not change any advisory. Every color in the archive was called on a counted plate, before this page existed.</li>
<li>It is not a reason to row on a red day. The model says the window can be longer than 48 hours, never shorter than what a reading says.</li>
</ul>
<p>What would make it real is a measurement: sampling this dock every few hours through the two days after one overflow, and fitting &lambda; to the observed decay. That is one hard weekend with a cooler, and it is the next thing on the list.</p>

<h2 id="reproduce">Reproducing this</h2>
<p>The whole model is one Python file, <span class="mono">analysis/loading-model.py</span>, about three hundred lines with the sources named in the comments beside the numbers they belong to. It uses nothing but the standard library, it needs no internet, and it writes both figures on this page. Running <span class="mono">python3 loading-model.py</span> prints every table in this text. If you change a parameter and get a different answer, that is the point of shipping it.</p>

